Dear Tom,

Thank you again for coming back to me and for being willing to explore this=
 =E2=80=94 I really appreciate it.

I realise there has been a misunderstanding, so I wanted to clarify the req=
uest briefly and clearly, and to make this easy for you to forward if helpfu=
l.

I am not seeking best-practice guidance or forward-looking recommendations =
on reasonable adjustments.=20

=20

That clinical work has already been completed by the NHS Central London Adu=
lt Autism Service (CLAAS).=20

=20

As CLAAS expressly state, their assessment and supporting letter are clinic=
al and are not intended to provide expert evidence or opinions on legal proc=
ess, effectiveness, or compliance.

=20

For the purposes of the Judicial Review, I am seeking an independent expert=
, instructed via solicitors, to provide a retrospective, evaluative opinion =
based on evidence. In outline, the expert would be asked to:

=20

=C2=B7         review a structured, contemporaneous dataset of administrative c=
orrespondence and metadata (approximately February=E2=80=93June 2024),

=C2=B7         assess whether reasonable participation measures were in place d=
uring the proceedings for the relevant period (February=E2=80=93June 2024),

=C2=B7         consider how participation needs and any adjustments were addres=
sed in practice over time,

=C2=B7         assess effectiveness in practice (as distinct from theoretical a=
vailability),

=C2=B7         consider whether adjustments were withdrawn or inconsistently ap=
plied, and provide an opinion on the cumulative impact of these matters on e=
ffective participation.

=20

This is not a request for advocacy, policy work, legal conclusions, or clin=
ical diagnosis =E2=80=94 but rather an independent expert assessment within the ex=
pert=E2=80=99s professional domain.

To make this easier, I=E2=80=99ve attached a short one-page summary of the propos=
ed expert instruction, which you are very welcome to forward to any contacts=
 if that is helpful.=20

Thank you again for your openness and engagement.

With my kind wishes,
Nadia Zahmoul

=20

On 23/01/2026, 8:12=E2=80=AFAM, "Tom Purser" <Tom.Purser@autismaction.org.uk> wro=
te:

=20

Hello Nadia

=20

Let me ask a few people - I don=E2=80=99t think this is an especially common =E2=80=98s=
ervice=E2=80=99 provided so it would be a case of identifying an expert with parti=
cular interest or insight in this specific area. To clarify, you are looking=
 for someone to:

=20

prepare a report for you
setting out best practice and recommendations for reasonable adjustments to=
 court proceedings
such that the court can follow these to best enable your participation in t=
hese proceedings
=20

Is this correct? If so, let me speak to a few connections and see if I can =
identify someone.=20

=20

Best wishes

=20

Tom

=20

From: Nadia <nadia@rosekross.com>
Sent: Thursday, January 22, 2026 18:12
To: Tom Purser <Tom.Purser@autismaction.org.uk>
Subject: Re: Important Update =E2=80=94 Judicial Review filed re Autism Act, Equa=
lity Act and PSED=20

=20

Dear Tom,

I hope you are well.

I wanted to thank you again for your guidance and for the thoughtful direct=
ion you have previously given me.

It has been genuinely helpful as the Judicial Review has progressed.

I now have a further, practical question and wondered if you might be willi=
ng to offer some guidance.

I am in the process of instructing an independent expert for the JR, to pro=
vide a court-ready report analysing effective participation and reasonable a=
djustments for an autistic court user, based on a longitudinal review of adm=
inistrative correspondence.

This evidence is likely to be pivotal to the court=E2=80=99s understanding of the=
 issues.

I have found that many experts will only accept instructions via lawyers (I=
 have now signed paperwork to instruct solicitors), but I wondered whether y=
ou might be able to recommend individuals or organisations who are credible,=
 independent, and experienced in this space =E2=80=94 for example psychologists or=
 disability-participation specialists who have acted as expert witnesses.

I completely understand the limits of what you and the organisation can do,=
 and I am not asking for case-specific support =E2=80=94 simply any pointers, name=
s, or agencies that you think might be appropriate.

Thank you again for your time and for everything you do in this area. I wil=
l of course keep you updated as things develop.

With kind wishes,
Nadia Zahmoul

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20

On 19/12/2025, 1:58=E2=80=AFPM, "Tom Purser" <Tom.Purser@autismaction.org.uk> wro=
te:

=20

Hello Nadia,

=20

Sorry it has taken me a while to come back to you.

=20

I hope that your claim is proceeding as you hoped.  In terms of answers to =
your questions, I would suggest:

=20
Leigh Day solicitors: I'm not sure if they have the direct experience of th=
e issues you are dealing with but they do a lot of work around autism
Slater Heelis Solicitors: UK Law Firm With 250 Years Of Experience: have lo=
ts of experience around family court issues Slater Heelis Solicitors=20
Doughty Street - Dr Oliver Lewis | Doughty Street Chambers
Black Letter Law=C2=AE
=20

On your second point, I'm afraid I'm no less clear as to how this might hap=
pen.  I think one of the challenges is that the legal circumstances around y=
our situation, from what I understand, are quite specific which may make it =
difficult to generalise principles from.  Someone who might take an interest=
 could be Sir Robert Buckland - ex-MP, ex-Minister, advocate around autism a=
nd, crucially, ex-Solicitor General.  More generally we are finding it quite=
 difficult to get autism-related issues to have a good hearing from this Gov=
ernment.  Depending on the outcome of your legal proceedings, and the partic=
ular findings and whether that creates a precedent, it may be that there is =
a broader principle we can help communicate, especially in our work around t=
he refresh of the autism strategy, but without knowing the outcome or the po=
ints of law it might turn on, I can't really commit to anything right now.

=20

So, you're very welcome to keep me updated and I'll always be honest in my =
responses to you.  I hope that you get the resolution you are seeking.

=20

Best wishes

=20

Tom

From: Nadia <nadia@rosekross.com>
Sent: 02 December 2025 10:09 AM
To: Tom Purser <Tom.Purser@autismaction.org.uk>
Subject: Important Update =E2=80=94 Judicial Review filed re Autism Act, Equality=
 Act and PSED

=20

Dear Tom,

Thank you again for speaking with me earlier this year, and for your though=
tful email in April. I appreciated the time you took to understand my experi=
ence, and I completely understand the limitations you described regarding th=
e charity=E2=80=99s capacity and areas of work.

=20

I am writing now with a significant update.

=20

Since our conversation, I have formally filed a Judicial Review in the High=
 Court, and the claim was sealed yesterday under case reference AC-2025-LON-=
004293. It appears=E2=80=94consistent with what you mentioned in your evidence to =
the Autism Act 2009 Committee=E2=80=94that this may be the first JR in the UK to d=
irectly challenge:
non-compliance with the Autism Act 2009 statutory guidance,
failures under the Equality Act 2010 and the Public Sector Equality Duty (P=
SED), and
systemic procedural barriers affecting autistic court users.
=20

Your remarks to Parliament about the absence of any legal challenge under t=
he Autism Act have stayed with me, and they were an important part of recogn=
ising that the issues I experienced needed to be addressed at a public-law l=
evel.

Given where the case now stands, I wondered whether you might be able to as=
sist in one or both of the following ways, in line with what you suggested b=
ack in April:

=20

1. Direction to individuals or organisations

=20

You mentioned that you might be able to point me toward people better place=
d to help. At this stage, I would be grateful for any referrals to:
lawyers or academics with expertise in autism and public law,
organisations familiar with systemic disability discrimination within the j=
ustice system, and/or
advocates who work on policy or media awareness relating to autistic court =
users.
=20

Even one or two names would be extremely helpful.

=20

2. Advice on the most effective way to ensure this case contributes to broa=
der change

=20

My hope=E2=80=94consistent with your comments to the Committee=E2=80=94is that this JR =
may help strengthen visibility and accountability around the Autism Act and =
its implementation by public bodies. If you have any insight on how best to =
ensure that the learning from my case feeds into wider awareness or policy w=
ork, I would be very grateful.

=20

I fully appreciate the charity=E2=80=99s limited capacity and I am not asking for=
 resources or case-specific support=E2=80=94only guidance, and if possible, connec=
tions to the right expertise.

=20

Thank you again for your time earlier this year, and for everything you do =
to improve the lives of autistic people. I would be grateful for any directi=
on you might be able to offer.

=20

With best wishes,

Nadia Zahmoul

Email: Nadia@rosekross.com

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20

=20


